Sector Leaders: Financials", "year": 2024, "rank": 17, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 78, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 93, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 238, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 180, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 71, "itemDescription": "The largest mutual life insurance provider by revenue in the U.S. came in 71st on the Fortune 500 after a year of steady growth.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>The insurance provider paid out a $2 billion dividend to clients in 2023, the largest in company history.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>CEO Craig DeSanto started his role as chief executive in April 2022, replacing Ted Mathas.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company reported $58.5 billion in revenue in 2022, up 14% from the year prior.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>New York Life saw a significant drop in profits, however, reporting a loss of more than $1 billion in 2022, as many insurance providers faced higher claims amid rising inflation.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 263, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 72, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">New York Life Insurance, the largest mutual life insurer in the U.S., saw strong continued demand for its products, which also include retirement income products, investment management services, and long-term care insurance. In 2021, its revenue increased 10% to $51.2 billion, and it swung from a loss in 2020 to a modest profit, as the company adjusted to life insurance payouts related to the COVID-19 pandemic.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 242, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;New York Life, which jumps six spots this year, was well prepared for COVID-19. After all, the 175-year-old company previously survived yellow fever, the Spanish flu, two world wars, and the Great Depression. Though revenues were up 5.9% in 2020, profits dropped sharply. Even so, in a bid to soothe employee anxiety, the company pledged in March 2020 there would be no layoffs through the end of the year. The company is also taking the lead in addressing the racial wealth gap, debuting a $1 billion impact investment initiative to invest in \u201cunderserved and undercapitalized communities over the next three years,\u201d it says. Investments will run the gamut from small businesses to affordable housing.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">New York Life, which jumps 28 spots on the Global 500 this year, was well prepared for COVID-19. After all, the 175-year-old company previously survived yellow fever, the Spanish flu, two world wars, and the Great Depression. Though revenues were up 5.9% in 2020, profits dropped sharply. Even so, in a bid to soothe employee anxiety, the company pledged in March 2020 there would be no layoffs through the end of the year. The company is also taking the lead in addressing the racial wealth gap, debuting a $1 billion impact investment initiative to invest in \u201cunderserved and undercapitalized communities over the next three years,\u201d it says. Investments will run the gamut from small businesses to affordable housing.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 67, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;New York Life, which jumps six spots this year, was well prepared for COVID-19. After all, the 175-year-old company previously survived yellow fever, the Spanish flu, two world wars, and the Great Depression. Though revenues were up 5.9% in 2020, profits dropped sharply. Even so, in a bid to soothe employee anxiety, the company pledged in March 2020 there would be no layoffs through the end of the year. The company is also taking the lead in addressing the racial wealth gap, debuting a $1 billion impact investment initiative to invest in \u201cunderserved and undercapitalized communities over the next three years,\u201d it says. Investments will run the gamut from small businesses to affordable housing.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">New York Life, which jumps six spots this year, was well prepared for COVID-19. After all, the 175-year-old company previously survived yellow fever, the Spanish flu, two world wars, and the Great Depression. Though revenues were up 5.9% in 2020, profits dropped sharply. Even so, in a bid to soothe employee anxiety, the company pledged in March 2020 there would be no layoffs through the end of the year. The company is also taking the lead in addressing the racial wealth gap, debuting a $1 billion impact investment initiative to invest in \u201cunderserved and undercapitalized communities over the next three years,\u201d it says. Investments will run the gamut from small businesses to affordable housing.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 270, "itemDescription": "In December New York Life Insurance entered into a $6.3 billion deal to acquire Cigna\u2019s group life and disability insurance business. Deals that large have been a rarity for the 175-year-old company. Already one of the nation\u2019s largest mutual life insurance firms, New York Life posted a 14% year-over-year profit increase in 2019. The company believes the coronavirus pandemic and its negative impact on the economy are pushing people to think more about their finances\u2014which could ultimately boost New York Life\u2019s insurance offerings."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 73, "itemDescription": "In December New York Life Insurance entered into a $6.3 billion deal to acquire Cigna\u2019s group life and disability insurance business. Deals that large have been a rarity for the 175-year-old company. Already one of the nation\u2019s largest mutual life insurance firms, New York Life posted a 14% year-over-year profit increase in 2019. The company believes the coronavirus pandemic and its negative impact on the economy are pushing people to think more about their finances\u2014which could ultimately boost New York Life\u2019s insurance offerings."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 268, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 71, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Compan